commit 5364272e1b
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 16 15:07:07 2026 +1000

    iLBC codec: Bound the iLBC frame count and handle decode errors

    The iLBC size query accepted any payload whose length was a multiple of
    38 or 50 bytes, but libilbc decodes only one to three frames per call
    and returns -1 otherwise. The -1 was stored in a size_t, so the decoder
    reported 2^64-2 bytes and the RTP player and sharkd read far past the
    output buffer.

    Return 0 for payloads of more than three frames (150 bytes) and treat a
    negative result from the library as no samples.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 24e3d327e00df32885ea3103dbc3c302a5242df8)

commit a6ed80f04e
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 16 15:07:07 2026 +1000

    Codecs: Size the G.726 output for bits carried between packets

    For 3 and 5 bits per sample (G726-24/-40, AAL2-G726-24/-40) spandsp
    keeps the leftover bits of one call and uses them in the next, so a
    packet can decode one more sample than inputBytes * 8 / bits. The size
    query did not account for it and spandsp wrote two bytes past the
    allocated buffer.

    Round the sample count up, which covers the at most bits-1 carried bits.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 38ffbd7d0e6647fe7db40fbeffa7f19a9e8e89a8)

commit d654a97c81
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 16 15:07:07 2026 +1000

    AMR, AMR-WB: Fix the length check for octet-aligned payloads

    decode_many checked the payload size by adding one TOC byte per frame
    already processed, but the speech data starts after all of the TOC
    entries. For frame i the check was short by frames-1-i bytes, so the
    memcpy of the frame read up to 31 (AMR) or 60 (AMR-WB) bytes past the
    payload.

    Count CMR and all TOC entries up front and add only the speech size per
    frame.

