commit 410db6e2d5
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 10 20:26:00 2026 +1000

    RDP: Bound channel PDU and ServerData allocations, fix leaks

    Every ServerData SC_NET and SC_MCS_MSGCHANNEL created new dissector
    handles with create_dissector_handle() on every pass, once per channel
    id, and never freed them. A capture with many ServerData PDUs listing
    thousands of channels used hundreds of megabytes, doubled with -2 and
    grew with each reload. The handles are now created once in the handoff
    routine, registered in the "t124.sd" table only on the first pass, and
    at most RDP_MAX_CHANNELS channel ids are registered per ServerData.

    The reassembly buffer of a virtual channel PDU was preallocated with
    the length announced by the first chunk, up to 4 GiB per frame in file
    scope. The array now grows with the data that is actually present.
    pendingLen is clamped instead of underflowing when a chunk is larger
    than the announced length.

    The negotiate request copied the server address into file scope on
    every pass; it is now done on the first pass only. The flags peeked in
    the licensing stage no longer leak into the virtual channel PDU path,
    where a channel length with bit 0x08 set was shown as encrypted.

    The tvb of a reassembled virtual channel PDU was created with
    tvb_new_real_data() on the first pass and kept in file scope, where it
    was never freed; every reassembled PDU leaked it, together with the
    subset tvbs that the channel dissectors created from it. Keep the
    reassembled data and its lengths instead, and build a child tvb of the
    packet on every pass.

    A multitransport link created from the MultiTransport request left its
    UDP conversation pointer uninitialized, and the lookup from a UDP
    conversation compared against it. Zero the links when allocating them.

    On 4.4 there is no channel PDU reassembly or ServerData chunk code, so
    only the zero-initialised transport links, flags initialisation,
    once-only t124.sd registration with reusable handles (bounded by
    RDP_MAX_CHANNELS) and first-pass-only server address update are ported.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 686feab83ecf3f9943a57c1e33e22b87615d96fb)

commit 32bf93511e
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 8 14:33:51 2026 +1000

    NMEA0183: Fix FT_CHAR handling and a few length errors

    The ETL number of shafts and XTR direction fields are FT_CHAR but were
    added through proto_tree_add_item_ret_string(), which reports a
    dissector bug for every such sentence. Add them as items instead.

    All 122 FT_CHAR fields were registered with BASE_NONE. A one character
    field holding a non-printable octet such as 0xbd then reached the
    "Invalid base" dissector bug when the label was formatted, outside of
    any exception handler, so printing the tree aborted (tshark -V). Use
    BASE_HEX, which shows such a character as '\xbd'.

    An empty or missing FT_CHAR (or numeric) field was added with a length
    of 0 from the packet, which throws and loses the rest of the sentence.
    Add a zero length item of the field type instead, and show only one
    byte, with an expert info, for an FT_CHAR field longer than one
    character.

    When '*' directly follows the sentence id the data length wrapped and
    the checksum was decoded as data; treat it as empty data. Check the
    binary file descriptor length before computing the length of the status
    field and add an expert info if it is too short. Use the length of the
    message, not its end offset, for the item of the second and later
    UdPbC sentences. Make the AAM units of radius field FT_CHAR with the unit names so that
    it shows the letter.

    The latitude and longitude direction fields were added with
    proto_tree_add_item_ret_string() and a NULL scope, which leaked the
    string; use pinfo->pool. decode_time() and decode_date() split the
    field at byte offsets, which cut a UTF-8 sequence when the field held a
    non-ASCII byte (shown as U+FFFD); reformat only fields of plain digits,
    and keep the whole first character of a unit label.

    On 4.4 only the direction field leak and the empty-data length wrap
    apply; the rest of the code touched upstream does not exist here.

    Assisted-by: Claude
