commit c6d4451aff
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Nov 13 13:32:44 2026 +1000

    WoWW: Fix CPU blow-ups on unterminated strings and packed GUIDs

    A C string without a terminating NUL in the next 32 KB made
    get_null_terminated_string_length() return 0, so add_cstring() did not
    advance the cursor. Loops driven by a packet supplied count then ran up
    to 2^32 times, each scanning 32 KB, and the AddonInfo loop in
    CMSG_AUTH_SESSION did the same over the decompressed data. Find the NUL
    with tvb_find_uint8() and, when there is none, consume the rest of the
    tvb so the item ends at the boundary or throws.

    add_packed_guid() and add_update_mask() created a child tvb and a data
    source for every GUID and every update mask. add_new_data_source()
    appends to a list, so a frame with many packed GUIDs cost quadratic
    time and one allocation per GUID. Add the GUID as a uint64 value over
    the bytes it occupies, and the update mask index as a uint value, with
    no extra data source.

    The packet size in add_header_to_tree() was a uint16_t and wrapped for
    large size fields; use unsigned.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit b58049b154f8e2639680b32d7392630ab456e8c0)
    [Backport: 4.4 allocates from wmem_packet_scope() instead of
    pinfo->pool, so add_update_mask() no longer uses pinfo and marks it
    _U_; get_null_terminated_string_length() uses tvb_find_uint8() because
    4.4 does not have tvb_find_uint8_remaining(). 4.4 also lacks e32674357c
    (packet-woww.c: Avoid use of uninitialized memory), so
