commit e359a11361
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Nov 13 13:32:43 2026 +1000

    Wi-Fi NAN: Bound nesting of vendor specific attributes

    The NAN Vendor Specific attribute passed the rest of the frame, not its
    own length, to the 802.11 vendor IE dissector, which reaches the NAN
    beacon dissector again. The outer loop then dissected the same bytes
    once more, so k back-to-back attributes took 2^k - 1 dissections and a
    single beacon exhausted CPU and memory. Nested attributes also
    recursed until the stack overflowed.

    Pass only attr_len bytes to the nested dissector, and count the nesting
    per packet with p_get_proto_depth(). Beyond 8 levels an expert info is
    added and the nested IE is skipped. The same guard is used for the
    Element Container attribute.

    Also fix the Extended Mesh attribute's mesh ID length, which mixed the
    attribute length with a frame offset; it is now attr_len - 13.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit b40d9f3dd79ce284530958af614dee673191f251)
    [Backport: 4.4 calls dissector_try_uint_new() instead of
    dissector_try_uint_with_data() and does not include epan/tfs.h or
    wsutil/array.h; kept those, added only epan/proto_data.h, and replaced
    the vendor specific IE tvb created with tvb_new_subset_length(..., -1).]

commit 0e1c6460fa
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Nov 13 13:32:43 2026 +1000

    WebSocket: Cap decompressed permessage-deflate output

    A permessage-deflate message was inflated with only a 2 GiB limit and
    the output was kept in file scope for later passes. Deflate expands
    about 1000:1, so a small capture of compressed frames exhausted memory
    with default settings. Stored output is now capped at 10 MiB per
    message. The rest is still inflated and discarded so that the sliding
    window stays in sync for later messages, and a new expert info reports
    the truncation.

