commit 78813a0084
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 11 05:03:02 2026 +1000

    SML: Fix endless loops from length underflow and backwards offset

    get_length() subtracted the number of length bytes from the decoded
    value without checking that the value was at least that large. A length
    byte of 0x00, or a multi-byte length with a small value, wrapped to a
    huge unsigned number. Loops over list entries then advanced by zero
    bytes up to 4G times, hanging tshark. Clamp the result to 0, so every
    field advances by at least its length bytes.

    In AttentionRes, a large attentionNo length moved the offset backwards
    into the previous message, so the message loop in dissect_sml_file()
    re-parsed it forever. Stop dissecting the message when the length is
    larger than the remaining data.

    Also pass &value_List_Entry to proto_tree_add_subtree_format() so that
    proto_item_set_end() on the entry actually does something.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit ae0165bea3de73aa627a5c13c5dd9ce46def623e)

commit dfa5a6406e
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 11 05:03:02 2026 +1000

    SMB2: Bound decompression and the redirect IP loop, free async tables

    Compression Pattern_V1 and the LZ payloads of a chained
    COMPRESSION_TRANSFORM were only limited to 16 MB per payload, so a
    44-byte PDU could allocate 32 MB, and every PDU of a TCP segment adds
    up in the same frame. Keep a running total of decompressed bytes per
    frame and refuse any payload that would take the total over 16 MB
    (MAX_UNCOMPRESSED_SIZE), for the chained, Pattern_V1, uncompressed and
    non-chained LZ paths.

    The asyncs and asy_cancels hash tables of a conversation were never
    destroyed when the conversation went away, which leaked on every
    capture with a STATUS_PENDING response. Destroy them in
    smb2_conv_destroy().

    An OriginalMessageSize of 0xFFFFFFFF in a transform header wrapped the
    offset, so the next message was dissected backwards. Clamp the advance
    to the data present. The create context NextEntryOffset was stored in
    a uint16_t and truncated; use uint32_t.

    The share redirect error context loops over an IP address count taken
    from the packet. For an unknown address family
    dissect_windows_sockaddr_storage() returns the offset minus one, so the
    offset moved back and forth and the loop ran up to 2^32 times; without
    a tree nothing stopped it and memory grew without bound. Stop with an
    expert info when an address does not move the offset forward.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit a9f4db7b6c0ce685767206d6079f8118b007800e)
