    returns uint8_t * here) and took master's uint32_t chain_offset;; this
    branch's smb2_conv_info_t has no asyncs or asy_cancels tables, so their
    destruction in smb2_conv_destroy() was dropped; otherwise identical.]

commit 4346061bff
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 11 05:03:02 2026 +1000

    SigComp: Fix UDVM memory and loop bounds

    With the sigcomp.decomp.msg preference on, the state/execution trace
    data source was created over the UDVM memory with a length of state
    address plus state length, which can reach 0x1FFFE bytes while the
    memory is 65536 bytes; showing the bytes read past the heap buffer.
    Fail the state access when the state does not lie within the UDVM
    memory.

    The reference operand decoder read buff[65536] for a 0xC0 operand at
    the last address. Wrap the address instead.

    STATE-ACCESS read the partial identifier from UDVM memory only up to
    the end of memory and then used uninitialised stack bytes. Wrap the
    address like the rest of the UDVM does.

    COPY-OFFSET was charged one cycle for a loop of up to 65535 steps,
    which lets a small message burn far more than the cycle budget.
    Charge the offset to the cycle count; the existing check before the
    next instruction then fails the decompression.

    The decompressed message is passed to SIP, which hands data starting
    with 0xF8 back to SigComp, so bytecode that outputs its own message
    recursed until the layer limit assertion. Limit the SigComp to SIP
    nesting to 4 levels, with an expert info, and count the dissection
    depth.

    Smaller fixes: the SHA-1 instruction wrote only every other digest
    byte (n was incremented twice) and its copy loop used the wrong start
    and length for the split at byte_copy_right and rejected a copy ending
    exactly at the end of memory (now like the CRC loop); literal operands
    masked the 10nnnnnn form with 0x1f instead of 0x3f and decoded the
    11000000 form from the wrong bytes; INPUT-HUFFMAN accepted bits_n up
    to 31, shifting a 16 bit value out of range, now limited to 16 as in
    RFC 3320.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 4ca0547ed3927c209ec82776b0fa35156e64303b)
    [Backport: this branch's val_to_str() takes no allocator, so the new
    diagnostic uses the three argument form; otherwise identical.]

commit 3f756eb028
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 11 05:03:02 2026 +1000

    SEL Protocol: Fix an endless loop in Read Response dissection

    A Data Format Response can announce a quantity of 0xFFFF values for a
    data item. dissect_fastmsg_readresp_frame() used 16-bit variables for
    the loop counter and the byte count, so the counter wrapped and
    "cnt <= quantity" was always true, while 2 * 0xFFFF wrapped so that the
    per-value length became zero and the offset never advanced. Without a
    tree this hung tshark at 100% CPU. Use a 32-bit counter and data size.

    The first pass of dissect_selfm() reused "offset" while it stored the
    Data Format Response and Device Description items, so the following
    function code checks read the wrong byte and the tree was built from a
    wrong offset, showing malformed packets on a one-pass run. Use a
    separate offset for those loops and drop the first-pass call to the
    read response dissector, which the tree path runs on every pass anyway.

    Shifting the 32-bit unsolicited SER status word by 32 or more is
    undefined; report those elements as not asserted. Make the raw 16-bit
    analog value field FT_INT16 so negative values are shown as negative.

    Data item, data region and analog channel names are copied from the
    packet into fixed buffers and shown in item labels, which must be valid
    UTF-8. Replace octets that are not ASCII.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 54668b1059684ced253efbef3aa46c212519d5e4)

commit 9635fa57e6
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 11 05:03:01 2026 +1000

    SCCP: Fix an infinite loop in XUDT/LUDT optional parameter scanning

    The variable and optional pointers in dissect_xudt_common() and
    dissect_sccp_message() were uint16_t. When the tvb is longer than 65535
    bytes (reachable through wtap encapsulation or Exported PDU), the loop
    that skips optional parameters looking for SEGMENTATION or END wrapped
    the pointer back into the buffer before tvb_get_uint8() could throw, and
    never terminated. Use unsigned for the pointers so the scan runs off the
    end of the tvb and throws.

    Also make the length_offset argument of sccp_reassemble_fragments()
    unsigned so LUDT data offsets above 65535 are not truncated, clamp the
    parameter length used with the dt1_ignore_length preference to 16 bits
    instead of truncating, and in looks_like_valid_sccp() reject pointers
    equal to the length, since reading the length byte there would throw
    from a heuristic that must not.

