commit 5201798de0
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 11 05:03:01 2026 +1000

    RTP: Fix RFC 2198 recursion, uninitialized info and A2DP payload length

    A RFC 2198 block whose payload type is again RFC 2198 (payload type 99
    by default, or any "red" dynamic type) was dispatched back to the RED
    dissector with only one byte consumed per level, until the generic
    layer limit assertion fired. Track the nesting with a per-packet depth
    counter and, for a block inside a block, show an expert info
    (rtp.rfc2198_nested) instead of recursing.

    dissect_rtp_rfc2198() filled its copy of struct _rtp_info only when
    called with data, but passed it to every block's payload dissector.
    With NULL data (Exported PDU) AMR or EVS read uninitialized pointers.
    Zero-initialize the copy and pass NULL on to the payload dissectors when
    there was no RTP info.

    For A2DP and VDP streams info_payload_len was decremented once per
    codec or SCMS-T header byte with no lower bound, so an RTP packet
    without payload wrapped it to 0xFFFFFFFF, which the RTP player then used
    as a copy length. Compute the length from the final payload offset, as
    the padding case already does, so it is clamped at zero.

    Also dissect the RTP header of PacketCable CCC after the 12 byte CCC
    header instead of from the start of the CCC header.

