commit 3a26f371b2
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 10 20:25:59 2026 +1000

    RPC: Fix type confusion on reused XIDs and an SRT tap overflow

    A call whose XID was already in the conversation's table reused the
    existing call info even when program, version or procedure differed.
    The dissector was chosen from the new call but received the old
    call's private data, which another program may have filled with an
    integer (portmap GETPORT stores the protocol number there). An NFS
    ACCESS reply then dereferenced it as a pointer and crashed. The same
    happened through the portmap CALLIT path. Only reuse the entry when
    program, version and procedure match, otherwise treat the packet as a
    new call. The table is updated only on the first pass; later passes
    use a packet-scoped copy. For indirect (CALLIT) calls a mismatching
    entry is never replaced, so later passes match replies the same way
    as the first one.

    The SRT tap compared the procedure with a signed cast, so a procedure
    of 0x80000000 or more became a negative index into the procedure
    table. Compare unsigned.

    Also: fix the mask in dissect_rpc_authgss_context (0xffffffc masked
    the top bits), set the GSS context create and destroy frames only on
    the first pass, and make the length in find_and_dissect_rpc_fragment
    signed so a "need more data" result is not corrupted.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 571c325c8f01afc67ac63ebebb5fc818fd1a80c9)
    [Backport: the find_and_dissect_rpc_fragment() hunks conflicted with
    master's earlier unsigned offset rework, not on this branch; this
    branch's len is already int, so kept its code there; otherwise
    identical.]

commit 8cbfb413dc
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 8 18:54:24 2026 +1000

    nordic_ble: Do not mix proto data types under one key

    dissect_ble_delta_time() stored an integer and dissect_ble_timestamp()
