commit 306b52a569
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 4 13:47:44 2026 +1000

    SMB: Fix NT Create AndX, two-pass state and quadratic FID lookups

    The NT Create AndX response with an extended word count (42 or more)
    assigned the return value of dissect_smb_access_mask_bits() to the
    offset. That function returns 0 for the generated "Granted Access
    Rights" item, so the offset was reset to 0, the byte count was read
    from the SMB marker and the body was shown as extra bytes or, with
    an AndX chain, as malformed. Do not assign the return value.

    Matches found through ct->primaries (secondary Transaction requests and
    the final Transaction response) were not added to ct->matched, so on
    later passes (tshark -2, the GUI) the secondaries showed "Continuation
    of: <unknown>" and the response lost its "Response to" and time fields
    and its Trans2 sub-command. Add them to ct->matched on the first pass.

    pdata->multi_cmds was set during the first pass from the number of
    commands seen so far and was therefore different on later passes,
    which changed the Info column with -2. Set it for every pass from
    the position of the SMB in the frame, in one place, for all readers.

    The TID table used wmem_tree_lookup32_le() on the TID and replaced the
    entry when a TID was reused, so an unmapped TID showed another share
    and later passes showed the later share for earlier frames. Key the
    table on {tid, frame} and check the stored TID. The FID list was kept
    sorted with g_slist_insert_sorted() and walked linearly on every lookup,
    which is quadratic in the number of Create responses. Use a wmem_tree
    keyed on {tid, fid, opened_in} with lookup32_array_le() and a check of
    the stored tid and fid, export the lookup as smb_fid_tree_lookup()
    and use it in MS-WSP, which walked the list too, and remove the
    list. Drop "|| !si->sip" from the FID lookup, which made the result
    of later passes differ from the first pass.

    Export Objects could write uninitialized heap memory into an exported
    file: after a chunk too far into the file made the buffer be dropped,
    the next chunk allocated a new buffer but did not copy into it, and the
    parts of a file between chunks were never initialized. Do not start a
    new buffer once the content was dropped, and zero the buffer when it is
    allocated or grown.

    Export Objects looped over g_slist_nth_data() in find_incoming_file(),
    insert_chunk() and smb_eo_cleanup(), which is cubic with many files.
    Iterate the list nodes directly.

    Smaller fixes: the "Requested Dialects" subtree length was always 0;
    Find Close Data Length was read big endian; the Read response gave
    dissect_file_data_maybe_dcerpc() a FID of 0 because the request info was
    looked up afterwards; the Delete request allocated file scope memory on
    every dissection; Write AndX with the pipe write length lacked a byte
