commit 8d291dd887
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:49:37 2026 +1000

    FTP: Bound the working directory and fix several smaller problems

    The conversation's working directory was deep-copied into the packet
    data of every request and response, and a CWD could grow the path
    without limit. A capture with many long CWD commands therefore used
    memory proportional to packets times path length (gigabytes for a
    1 MB capture). Limit the stored path to 4096 bytes, keep the old path
    and add an expert info when a CWD, CDUP or PWD would exceed it, and
    share one immutable string between the packets instead of copying it.
    Adding a path to an empty working directory no longer modifies a
    string that earlier packets and data conversations already point to.

    The expert info for a bad EPRT or 229 argument started at
    "offset - linelen - 1", which wraps around as an unsigned value and
    throws an exception. Use the start of the line.

    The invalid PWD response expert info was added only in the first pass.
    Check the line in every pass and update the conversation only in the
    first one.

    The Export Objects tap copied the reported length of the data from the
    tvbuff, which throws on a truncated capture. Use the captured length.

    The data stream duration and bit rate were computed with 32 bit
    arithmetic that could overflow and divided by zero for a zero or
    negative gap. Use 64 bit values and show them only for a positive gap.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit d41b724587d9c84fdcc556bcf51f03fdcd5d636c)

    (cherry picked from commit 8283f7eb5c0d0c7325a0765846f44563003ed0b4)

    Backport: Thread packet_info through older path helpers and use captured export lengths.

    Assisted-by: Codex

commit 3f0a75d284
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 4 17:22:29 2026 -0500

    EAP: Fix a zero-width AT_RES assert, the TEAP recursion and lengths

    An EAP-AKA AT_RES attribute with a RES Length of 0 was passed to
    proto_tree_add_bits_item() with a width of 0 bits, which is reported
    as a dissector bug. Flag the length as bad and skip the RES instead.

    EAP-TEAP outer TLVs and the tunnelled TLS data were handed to the
    TEAP and TLS dissectors without counting the dissection depth, so an
    EAP-Payload TLV that holds another EAP-TEAP message recursed until the
    layer limit was hit. Count the depth around both calls.

    Fix some lengths as well:
    - EAP-SAKE compared an absolute offset with a remaining length, so the
      last attributes were not shown. Pass the end offset instead.
    - The MS-Authentication-TLV loop made the same mix-up, so only the
      first TLV was shown. Track the end offset, check the value length
      against what is left, and move to the next TLV by its length.
    - The LEAP name length was truncated to 8 bits and could wrap; use an
      unsigned and clamp it at 0.
    - The EAP-MD5 extra data length was computed before Value-Size was
      clamped, so it wrapped when Value-Size was too big.

