commit 24f3f94145
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 10 15:16:32 2026 +1000

    PFCP: Bound grouped IE nesting and fix session tracking costs

    Grouped IEs called back into the IE dissector with no limit on the
    depth. A single UDP datagram with thousands of nested grouped IEs
    (3GPP or vendor/BBF) overflowed the stack when no tree was built.
    Stop at a nesting of 16 with a new expert info.

    dissect_pfcp_grouped_ie_wrapper() dereferenced its data argument, which
    is NULL when the vendor IE dissectors are called from Exported PDU.
    Return 0 in that case.

    The session tracking map was hashed on the SEID only and pfcp_fill_map()
    inserted the cross product of all SEIDs and addresses of a message, so
    a message with many F-SEID IEs took cubic time. Hash the address too and
    collect at most 64 SEIDs and 64 addresses per message, with an expert
    info when the limit is hit.

    Also fix several small bugs: the Remote GTP-U Peer network instance
    offset was advanced twice, pfcp_seid_exists() compared only 32 bits of
    the SEID, the F-SEID session lookup used a pointer as the key, the IE
    length parameter of dissect_pfcp_ies_common() was truncated to 16 bits,
    the BBF NAT port forward text always showed the first entry's address,
    and the SDF filter SMMII loop did not move to the end of each instance.

    The Nokia User Location Information IE called a "gtpv2.uli" dissector
    handle that was never registered, so every such IE hit the
    DISSECTOR_ASSERT in call_dissector() with default settings. Call the
    exported dissect_gtpv2_uli() instead. Also show the Source IP Address
    mask/prefix length as a number instead of reading 16 bytes as an IPv6
    address at the 1-byte field.

