commit 145edcae28
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:49:38 2026 +1000

    GIOP: Fix unbounded work and two-pass differences

    The request list was a GList that was searched linearly for every
    request and reply, so a capture with many requests took quadratic time
    (40k requests and replies took about 100 s). Replace it with wmem_maps
    keyed by frame number and by request id, source address and port. The
    latest request still wins, as before.

    A frame with the ZIOP flag was inflated with no limit on the output, so
    a small packet could allocate hundreds of megabytes. Inflate with a
    local zlib loop that stops when the result would exceed the
    giop.max_message_size preference and reports an expert info.

    Reading the stringified IOR file (giop.ior_txt) called decode_IOR() with
    a NULL pinfo, which dereferenced pinfo->pool and crashed in the init
    routine. Use a dummy packet_info with a per-line scratch allocator
    (frame number 0, so expert infos are dropped) and catch exceptions.

    The repository id of a request was looked up in the object key hash on
    every pass, but the hash is filled from IORs seen later in the file, so
    a second pass could show different results than the first. Resolve it
    on the first pass and keep it. Do the same for the message type of the
    last fragment, and only update the request id to message type map on
    the first pass.

    The heuristic sub dissector probes were run without a TRY, so one probe
    that threw ended the search and marked the message malformed. Catch the
    exception and go on with the next sub dissector.

    Smaller fixes: tk_ulong and tk_ulonglong values did not get their text
    because the item was not assigned; the wchar length was an int8_t; the
    offset-progress checks in the struct, sequence, array and union loops
    did not update the old offset; sequence, array and value typecode
    parameters read fields inside an encapsulation with the outer byte
    order and boundary; the proto depth is reset at the start of each PDU in
    case an exception left it raised; the last-fragment override stored the
    message size in host order where the header holds wire order; format_text
    was given a length that included the terminating NUL; LocateReply
    exception and addressing disposition were added to the wrong tree and a
    LocateRequest length error was attached to the wrong item.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
