commit 3eac92dd69
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:49:38 2026 +1000

    GeoNetworking: Fix IntX assertions, duplicate flag and lat/lon

    A security IntX field longer than four bytes made the dissector add a
    70-bit value to a 32-bit application ID field, and a 64-bit value to the
    32-bit local region field, so both ended in a bit width or type
    assertion. Fields longer than four bytes now only get the too-big
    expert info, and the PSID name is only shown for the application ID.

    The duplicate packet detection added its tree item, expert info and
    Info column text only on the first pass, so they were lost on a second
    pass such as with tshark -2. The result is now stored per packet with
    p_add_proto_data() on the first pass and shown on every pass.

    The latitude and longitude display functions multiplied the int32 value
    by 6, which overflowed, and used abs() on INT32_MIN. Compute them with
    64-bit values.

    IS_HT_KNOWN() lacked outer parentheses, so !IS_HT_KNOWN(x) only negated
    its first term and header types 0x43 and 0x33 were decoded misaligned.
    Add the parentheses, and accept the GeoAnycast sub-types (0x31, 0x32),
    which the check hidden by the missing parentheses rejected. The length
    counters of the certificate, signer, header field, recipient info and
    trailer loops are unsigned and were decremented by the size of each
    element, which wrapped when an element was longer than the declared
    length; stop the loop at zero instead.

