commit 8fc9aa3fe4
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Nov 7 21:58:57 2026 -0500

    DVB-CI: Fix CA PMT loop, two-pass state and length checks

    dissect_es() returned -1 for an elementary stream with a descriptor
    that is not a CA descriptor, and the CA PMT loop stored it in an
    unsigned variable. The "<= 0" check missed it and the offset moved
    back by one, so the same bytes were dissected forever when there was
    no tree. dissect_es() now returns 0 on error.

    An open session response created a new conversation on every pass. On
    the second pass each one was inserted behind later ones, which took
    quadratic time and doubled the conversations. Create it on the first
    pass only; later passes find it.

    The CI+ X.509 extensions were enabled around the certificate
    dissection without TRY/FINALLY, so a malformed certificate left them
    enabled, and certificates of other protocols were then decoded with
    the CI+ meaning of these OIDs. Disable them in a FINALLY block.

    The negotiated buffer size and the CAM's proposal were global state
    updated on every pass, so the "Payload too large" and buffer size
    checks differed between one and two passes. Keep the values that apply
    to a frame with p_add_proto_data(). The "Payload too large" expert
    info started at offset 2 with the length of the whole LPDU, threw an
    exception and stopped the reassembly; it now covers the LPDU.

    A hostname connection descriptor, or one of another type, whose length
    was too short and a CA descriptor shorter than 4 bytes gave a negative
    length, so the field ran to the end of the APDU and the offset moved
    back. They now get an expert info and the dissection stops.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>

    (cherry picked from commit 9c8b34ab3a8716f37fc543c0aacde92508d6629c)

