commit e46ae62003
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 8 12:57:19 2026 +1000

    Netlink: Bound message length and attribute nesting

    A netlink message length close to 2^32 made pkt_end_offset wrap, which
    was also a signed int, so the loop in dissect_netlink() moved backwards
    and dissected the same messages forever, allocating memory until it ran
    out. A message longer than the remaining data now gets an expert info
    (netlink.bad_length) and is clamped to the remaining length, so the loop
    always moves forward; the offsets are unsigned.

    Attribute callbacks could recurse without limit, each level costing only
    four bytes of packet, which overflowed the stack. Add a nesting counter
    and the packet_info to struct packet_netlink_data and stop in
    dissect_netlink_attributes_common() at 32 levels with an expert info
    (netlink.nesting_too_deep); each level also counts as a dissection
    level. This bounds net_dm (HW_ENTRIES/HW_ENTRY), ovs_flow (CLONE,
    SAMPLE, ENCAP, CHECK_PKT_LEN), ovs_packet and nl80211 (IFTYPE_EXT_CAPA)
    in one place. The structure is created only in dissect_netlink() and
    lives for one message, so an exception leaves no stale state.

