commit d752506abb
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Nov 7 12:30:39 2026 +1000

    LBMC: Fix memory blow-ups, NULL address and fragment handling

    dissect_lbmc_message_data() allocated a 64 KiB
    lbmc_extopt_reassembled_data_t and cleared it for every LBMC message in
    a packet, so a frame of 4-byte messages took over 1 GB. The structure
    is now allocated once per call and only its small header fields are
    reset per message.

    The reassembly, stream and substream key builders copied four bytes
    from address.data, which is NULL for AT_NONE addresses (Exported PDU
    without IP tags). One helper now returns 0 unless the address has data
    of at least four bytes.

    dissect_msg_properties() trusted the key and value offsets, so all
    fields could point at one long string and multiply the work. The
    offsets must now lie after the field table and increase from field to
    field, otherwise an expert info is added and dissection stops. The
    string size lookups now include the base offset.

    lbmc_message_add_fragment() rejects overlapping fragments, which left
    uninitialized bytes in the reassembled message, and the reassembly
    buffer is now zeroed. The duplicate check comes before the length
    checks so that a second pass (tshark -2) accepts the fragments it has
    already stored. The reassembled tvb is now a child tvb of the LBMC tvb
    instead of a real-data tvb that was never freed.

    The UMQ index command item lengths used index_len before it was
    assigned, and the application header chain used a uint8_t length that
    could wrap; it is now an int and the loop stops when an element is
    longer than the remaining data.

    The UMQ index command release and reserve headers computed the index
    length without the base command header, unlike the responses, so every
    one of them was malformed. Subtract the base header too, and compute the
    length before the item that uses it.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 5f6631e1cfcafaae29f5e6207b0f6cf8afe8c84e)
    [Backport: adapted to 4.4's unbased message-properties key/value offsets (used the fix's offset+key_offset / offset+value_offset form and dropped the then-unused actual_key_offset).]

commit 63218980aa
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Nov 7 21:00:00 2026 +1000

    MiWi: Fix buffer overflow, address map and payload offsets

    miwi_short_to_str() writes "Broadcast" (10 bytes) for 0xffff, but the
    destination and source PAN id and short address strings were kept in
    8-byte wmem_new(uint64_t) buffers. This overflows the heap buffer for
    any frame with a broadcast address. Use 16-byte local arrays at all four
    sites.

    The miwi_map hash tables were never created, so every lookup and
    address update ended in a GLib critical. Create them in an init routine
    and destroy them in a cleanup routine, like packet-ieee802154.c does for
    its map. With the tables in place, fix the code that was unreachable
    before: add the inferred extended source address with
    hf_miwi_ext_src_addr instead of the FT_UINT16 short address field, and
    update the address map only on the first pass.

    Also hand only the data after the header to the data dissector for
    unknown commands and for data frames with a bad FCS, instead of the
    whole frame from offset 0.

