commit 4f4f7863db
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 22:09:48 2026 +1000

    H.265: Fix palette initializer and bit width handling

    The SPS screen content coding extension used (1 << BitDepth) - 1 as the
    width of each palette predictor initializer instead of BitDepth. For
    8-bit video this asked for 255 bits and raised a dissector bug, and for
    a bit depth of 32 the width wrapped to 0 so the loop made no progress
    while running up to 3 * 2^32 iterations. Use BitDepthY/BitDepthC as the
    width, and stop with an expert info if bit_depth_*_minus8 is larger than
    8 or sps_num_palette_predictor_initializers_minus1 is 128 or more.

    The PPS screen content coding extension decoded
    pps_num_palette_predictor_initializers as a signed Exp-Golomb value into
    an unsigned field, which asserted, and mixed byte and bit offsets so the
    loop re-read the same bits. Decode it as ue(v), keep a single bit
    offset, and stop with an expert info if the count is above 128 or a bit
    depth entry is above 8.

    lt_ref_pic_poc_lsb_sps used log2_max_pic_order_cnt_lsb_minus4 + 4 as a
    bit width without a bound; reject values above 12 in the SPS.

    hrd_layer_set_idx is read with ue(v) but registered as FT_UINT8, which
    asserted. Make it FT_UINT32.

    Smaller fixes: vps_num_units_in_tick and vps_time_scale are 32-bit
    fields, vps_time_scale was added with a length of 1 bit; the sub layer
    profile_idc was read at a byte offset used as a bit offset; the sub
    layer profile compatibility flags were all read from the same bit; the
    FU header NAL unit type uses six bits, not five.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit ee78dc15450af59743ab0c5f79092cee18d74500)
    [Backport: the SCC extension signatures were made unsigned to match the
    fix, and their forward declarations updated to match. 4.4 has no
    h265_type_values_ext / h265_type_summary_values_ext, so the unrelated
    NAL unit type extended-value-string change was dropped and the type
    mask kept at 0x1f. The palette-width security fix, the bit-depth and
    initializer-count guards, the profile_tier bit offsets and the
    vps_time_scale width fix apply unchanged.]

commit 6eb38210ad
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 08:50:17 2026 +1000

    DVB-CI: Fix CA PMT length checks, two-pass state and CI+ cert leak

    A hostname connection descriptor, or one of another type, whose length
    was too short, and a CA descriptor shorter than 4 bytes, gave a negative
    length so the field ran to the end of the APDU and the offset moved
    back. They now get an expert info and the dissection stops.

    An open session response created a new conversation on every pass, which
    took quadratic time and doubled the conversations; create it on the
    first pass only. The negotiated buffer size and the CAM's proposal were
    global state updated on every pass, so the checks differed between one
    and two passes; keep the per-frame values with p_add_proto_data(). The
    CI+ X.509 extensions were enabled around the certificate dissection
    without TRY/FINALLY, so a malformed certificate left them enabled and
    other protocols' certificates were then decoded with the CI+ meaning of
    these OIDs; disable them in a FINALLY block.

    Assisted-by: Claude

    (backported from commit 9c8b34ab3a8716f37fc543c0aacde92508d6629c)
    [Backport: the upstream commit also fixes a CA PMT endless loop where
     dissect_es() returns the -1 error sentinel into an unsigned es_info_len,
     so "<= 0" misses it and the offset moves back. That is a master/5.0-only
     regression from 8638423a19 ("Use expert_remaning and unsigned offsets.",
     2026-01-05), which is not on release-4.4: here dissect_es() returns int,
     es_info_len/ca_desc_len are int and the "<= 0" checks still catch the -1,
     so there is no loop. dissect_es() and the CA PMT loop are therefore left
     unchanged; only the length checks, the two-pass state and the CI+
     certificate leak are backported.]

commit 71a3c59030
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 08:50:16 2026 +1000

    DO-IRP: Fix TCP length wrap, length checks and request tracking

    get_do_irp_message_len() added the 20-byte envelope to the 32-bit
    message length, so a length of 0xffffffec or more wrapped to a small
    value or 0. With 0, tcp_dissect_pdus() asked for one more segment over
    and over, so the rest of the stream was swallowed and dissection time
    grew quadratically with the number of segments. A length that wraps
    is now returned as a value below the envelope length, which
    tcp_dissect_pdus() reports as a bounds error.

    decode_generic_data(), decode_pk_data() and decode_string() read the
    length with tvb_get_int32() and returned len + 4 as an int, and the
    credential and signed-info items used cred_len + 4 and sig_len + 4.
    A length near 0xffffffff wrapped, so the item had the wrong length and
    the caller's offset moved by a few bytes or backwards. The lengths are
    now read with tvb_get_uint32() and checked against the remaining
    data; a length beyond the data is reported with the new expert info
    do-irp.length.invalid and dissection stops.

    The UDP reassembly stored the 32-bit request and sequence IDs in
    uint16_t variables, so fragments of different messages were reassembled
    together. They are now uint32_t.

    The response frame of a request was updated on every pass, so the
    "Response In" link could differ between passes. It is now set only on
    the first pass, to the first response.

    The body length of an unsupported response code is handled as
    unsigned, so a consumed length beyond the body length no longer gives
    a negative subset length.

    Every envelope cleared the Info column, so a TCP segment with several
    messages showed only the last one. The column is now cleared once per
    segment (and per UDP datagram), and the messages are separated by
    " | ".

    Over TCP, a segment shorter than the 20 octet envelope was rejected
    before tcp_dissect_pdus() could ask for more data, so a message whose
    first segment was that short was lost. Such a segment now only has its
    version checked, and tcp_dissect_pdus() reassembles the envelope.

    Over UDP, the expected fragment count was set after the first fragment
    had been added with more fragments to come, so a message sent as a
    single fragment with the TC flag set was never reassembled. A message
    that fits into one fragment is now added as the last fragment.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 44f8fdc52f871c6bac9781485fbaf06cfe98fe38)
    [Backport: two small context conflicts (a decode_string check_len call
    and the unhandled-bytes tvb_ensure_bytes_exist64 guard) resolved in
    favour of the fix; otherwise applies unchanged.]

commit 4b7a1aae4e
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Nov 7 21:00:01 2026 +1000

    NAS EPS: Bound Replayed NAS message container nesting, fix leaks

    The Replayed NAS message container IE of a Security mode complete
    message was dissected by calling the EMM message dissector again, and
    the inner message could itself carry another container. Five bytes per
    level were enough to recurse thousands of levels deep and overflow the
    stack. The specification allows a single level, so a container inside
    a container is now reported with an expert info and not dissected, and
    the nested call is wrapped in increment_dissection_depth() and
    decrement_dissection_depth().

    The EEA2 deciphering output buffer was a GByteArray handed to a tvb
    that never freed it, leaking the plaintext of every ciphered message on
    each pass. It is now allocated from pinfo->pool. The key GByteArray is
    also freed when the key preference is invalid or applied again.

    The Satellite operation parameters IE tested the SF waiting time flag
    twice; the estimated UL delivery time duration is now conditional on
    its own indicator.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 46db1c5f4d8ee608cdd541ab14cab10f6ef769ae)
