commit cf3813aa06
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:49:38 2026 +1000

    GCSNA: Fix endless loop in the service reject message

    The service reject dissector kept its bit offset in a uint16_t. The
    offset wrapped at 8192 bits for large packets, moved backwards and made
    the field loops run until the tree guards stopped them. Use uint32_t
    for the offset, the field count and the cause.

    The number of reserved bits before the encapsulated PDU was computed
    from the low two bits of the offset instead of the low three, so it
    could add a wrong number of bits or a whole byte when the offset was
    already aligned. Compute it modulo 8 and skip the field when it is
    empty.

    The Service Reject default case added the unsupported-reject data with
    a bit offset used as a byte offset, so cause values the dissector does
    not know were always malformed. Convert the bit offset to a byte offset.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 593b90fa2500a55e70f1d401ffd71378669671ce)

commit c948343111
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:49:37 2026 +1000

    FTDI MPSSE: Fix assertions reachable from crafted packets

    A data shifting command with a 16-bit length of 0xffff in byte mode
    needs a response of 0x10000 bytes. The response length was passed as
    uint16_t, wrapped to 0 and ended in DISSECTOR_ASSERT(response_length >
    0). Pass it as uint32_t, and ignore a zero length instead of asserting.

    A preliminary command record completed by reassembly in a later frame
    was never inserted in the command tree for that frame, so the second
    pass found a different record and hit an assertion. Insert the pointer
    when the completing frame differs from the one that created the record.

    A stale preliminary record left from another MPSSE instance (for
    example after the device was re-enumerated) tripped the cmd and
    response_length assertions. Treat a mismatching record as no longer
    preliminary and start a new one. On later passes, skip a missing or
    mismatching record instead of asserting, and drop the assertion on the
    preliminary flag when advancing to the last record.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit cc3997eaa51e0634e5260285d8f462e9b61bfc81)

commit a86db8569c
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:49:37 2026 +1000

    FTDI FT: Fix assertion in reassembly and repeated state updates

    The reassembly code asserted that the desegment offset set by the
    subdissector is larger than the bytes already reassembled. The MPSSE
    dissector can set a smaller offset when it scans past a bad command, so
    a crafted packet hit DISSECTOR_ASSERT. Treat such an offset as a
    continuation of the current reassembly instead.

    The control request records and the bitmode records were stored again
    on every pass over a frame. Only update them on the first pass.

    Frames that are only part of a reassembly showed the protocol "FTDI
    MPSSE" in the first pass, because the first pass hands them to the
    subdissector, and "FTDI FT" in the second pass, which does not. Set the
    protocol column to "FTDI FT" in the first pass when nothing in the frame
    was completed.

    Assisted-by: Claude
