commit 815b9889ad
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 16:52:54 2026 +1000

    ERF: Bound Anchor ID linking and check parent_section length

    Every record with an Anchor ID extension header walked the list of all
    earlier records with the same Host ID and Anchor ID, added a "Linked
    Frame" item for each and marked each one as depended upon. A file in
    which every record carries the same Anchor ID took quadratic time,
    and, because mark_frame_as_depended_upon() stores the links in the
    frame data, quadratic memory when the frames are kept (-2 or the GUI):
    20000 records (1.7 MB) used up 2 GB. Now a non-definition record links
    only to the anchor definition records, which are kept in a list of
    their own, and a definition record links to all records with the
    Anchor ID as before. At most 64 links are shown and marked per record;
    an expert info says when there are more.

    The Anchor ID hash table compared the IDs masked with the extension
    header type (ERF_EXT_HDR_TYPE_ANCHOR_ID, 18) instead of the Anchor ID
    mask, so two different IDs could compare equal. Use
    ERF_EHDR_ANCHOR_ID_MASK.

    A parent_section tag shorter than 4 bytes was not treated as
    truncated, and its Section ID was added with a length of -1 or less,
    which runs to the end of the record. Give parent_section an expected
    length of 4, so it is reported as a truncated tag like the others.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 5f20ba9a3b0ac32160ef262111e74e6f180d0e94)

commit 49721adcc4
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 16:52:54 2026 +1000

    EPL: Fix quadratic mapping handling and profile parser crashes

    Every SDO ObjectMapping was appended to the conversation's mapping list
    on every pass and the whole list was then sorted again, and each
    mapping registered a subtree index of its own that was never freed. A
    capture with many mappings took quadratic time and leaked a subtree
    index per mapping and pass. Record mappings only on the first pass,
    insert them at their sorted position, limit a list to 4096 entries
    (with an expert info for those left out) and use a single registered
    subtree for all PDOs.

    The SDO duplication table was searched in full for every frame that
    starts or ends a sequence, which is quadratic in the number of entries,
    and it was updated again on every pass, so later passes could mark
    frames as duplicates that were not. Key the table by source and
    destination node with one small table of sequence numbers per pair,
    which is cleared in one go, update it only on the first pass and keep
    the duplicated frame number in the per-packet data.

    The profile parser dereferenced a NULL string when an EDS file has no
    Description in [FileInfo], crashing when the preferences are applied,
    passed an uninitialised GError to g_key_file_load_from_file() and
    leaked the error, rejected an XDD file when an XPath expression
    matched nothing, read the value of XML attributes without children,
    and let the fields of one subobject carry over to the next one. Check
    the description and the attribute values, initialise and free the
    error, treat a missing node set as empty and reset the subobject for
    each element.

    Also decode Real64 values with an FT_DOUBLE field instead of the
    FT_FLOAT one, don't let the remaining PDO payload length wrap around
    when mappings overlap, show variable length types such as strings over
    the whole payload instead of with length 0, start each entry of a
    Write Multiple Parameter by Index response at its own offset, show the
    subindex instead of the index in its subindex field and determine
    whether the entry has a subindex correctly, and read the 8-bit
    subindex of a mapping in a Read Multiple Parameter by Index response
    as one byte.

    Changing the device profile table again after it had two entries for
    the same device type freed memory twice: the second entry is a shallow
    copy kept in the scope of the first, and drop_profiles() destroyed that
    scope once per entry and read the freed profile. Mark the copies and
    free only the profiles that own their scope.

    Free the buffer a mapping from a profile is read from, and the node
    ID string of a profile table entry, which were leaked each time the
    preferences were applied.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 9c73369fb4065926af634ecb4d0e6392b7e96df2)

