    BCMC flow discriminator of length 0 were added as bit fields of more
    than 64 or of 0 bits, which is a dissector bug. Skip padding of more
    than 64 bits and check the lengths.

    The bit offsets were kept in 16 bits, so in a message past 8 KiB they
    wrapped back to the start of the packet and the message loop started
    over until the tree item limit stopped it. Keep them in 32 bits.

    The supplemental channel loops of an active set record used the
    counter of the enclosing pilot loop, and the channel count started at
    65535 when it was not in the message, so they did not end. Give them
    their own counter and start the count at 0.

    Also read the rejected order type, the RSC mode indication and the
    fast call setup ORDQ at their own offsets, and widen the TMSI fields
    to the bits added.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (cherry picked from commit 112396cd011eb80e44d15652e42684d4ece7277e)

commit 001dddad90
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 30 10:33:00 2026 +1000

    BT SDP: Do not search the whole record for every attribute

    For a DID or HDP service record, dissect_sdp_service_attribute()
    walked all attributes of the record with findUintAttribute() for every
    attribute it dissected, so a single attribute list was quadratic in the
    number of attributes: a 64 KB Service Attribute Response took about six
    seconds per pass. Search only for the attributes that use the result:
    the DID Vendor ID and Product ID and the HDP Supported Features.

    The continuation state was saved as a hex string, and only as many
    characters as the state has octets were copied into the reassembly
    key, so states differing in their second half were taken for the same
    state. Save the octets instead.

