commit b6d2f85d79
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Oct 27 17:37:41 2026 +1000

    ANSI IS-637: Do not assert on User Data longer than its text

    The reserved bit count in User Data was never bounded. A subparameter
    longer than its text gave a count over 64 bits, which hit the
    no_of_bits <= 64 assertion in tvb_get_bits64() for GSM 7-bit text and
    reported a dissector bug for the other encodings. A text longer than
    its subparameter wrapped the unsigned count instead and read the
    following subparameters as text. Keep the count signed and report Short
    Data or Extraneous Data instead, in User Data, Multiple Encoding User
    Data and Service Category Program Data.

    Also fix some decoding errors:
    - A Multiple Encoding or Service Category Program Data text that starts
      on an octet boundary was read from one octet too late.
    - The teleservice and transport message lengths were truncated to 8
      bits, so messages over 255 octets were cut short.
    - The Broadcast flag of the last transport message was kept, so a
      teleservice message reached another way decoded its User Data as
      CMAS depending on which packet was dissected before.
    - A CMAS type 0 record of length 0 threw an exception instead of
      reporting Short Data.
    - The reserved bits at the end of CMAS User Data were shown one octet
      past it, so a CMAS message that ends with User Data threw an
      exception.

    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    Assisted-by: Claude
    (cherry picked from commit 0d2f9272b74314baed46af2cc10e53c90931d08a)

