commit 4d446ec04d
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 4 17:29:09 2026 +1000

    Dissectors: Handle a missing or unexpected data argument

    A crafted Exported PDU file can name any registered dissector
    (EXP_PDU_TAG_DISSECTOR_NAME) or heuristic dissector
    (EXP_PDU_TAG_HEUR_DISSECTOR_NAME), and Exported PDU then calls it with
    the data argument NULL (or, for a handle that also has a TCP heuristic,
    with a struct tcpinfo). Many dissectors assume that data is always the
    structure their usual parent passes. Calling every registered name and
    every heuristic this way showed 36 that dereference a NULL pointer, two
    that abort in ws_assert_not_reached() (XCP over TCP and UDP) and about
    100 that fail a DISSECTOR_ASSERT, all with default settings and only by
    opening a file.

    Return early (0 for a dissector, false for a heuristic) when the data a
    dissector needs is missing, instead of dereferencing it or asserting,
    and handle the few values derived from it that were asserted on (an
    unexpected port type in XCP, a missing conversation in H.223, iPerf3,
    ZMTP, MS Proxy and SOCKS, a short IE length in PFCP, an unexpected
    address type for the default TCP MSS). WebSocket uses its data as HTTP
    upgrade information only when HTTP, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 is the caller.
    The behaviour with the data the usual parent passes is unchanged.

    [The 4.4 branch does not have the Vertexcom HomePlug AV vendor dissector,
    the mac80211_hwsim and Open vSwitch netlink dissectors, XCP, the IEEE
    802.15.4 dissector that takes the FCS type as data, or the TCP default
    MSS, so those changes are not part of this backport. PEAP keys the EAP
    tvb with tls_group.]

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 1e2c175c21c8ced022763ae7e8c4f2a133913fac)

commit 0aede29dff
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 16:52:53 2026 +1000

    EBHSCR: Fix FlexRay tvbs backed by stack arrays and a LIN length

    The FlexRay frame and symbol packets built the 2-byte FlexRay header
    in an array on the stack and made a tvb of it that outlived the
    function, so fields and filters such as flexray[0:2] read stack memory
    after it was gone. The symbol packet tvb was also never freed. Take
    the header from pinfo->pool and make the symbol tvb a child of the
    packet tvb, so it is freed with it.

    A LIN payload of 1 byte made the data length wrap to 0xFFFFFFFF;
    report it as too short with a new expert info instead. The NMEA string
    is now limited to the captured payload, not the header length field.

