commit d2f322d11f
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:46:24 2026 -0500

    SCTP: Don't re-use fragmented DATA chunks in another reassembly

    If a DATA chunks are transmitted in such a way that a completed
    series would appear be enveloped in other, i.e., two DATA chunks
    with B/E bits 10 with no end chunk with B/E bits 01 between them,
    and vice versa, that is strictly illegal according to RFC 9260
    section 6.9:

    https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9260/#section-6.9-8.3.1

    In particular, if the chunks are received out-of-order with respect
    to TSN, this can cause a fragment's data to already be used for the
    apparent inner message and freed before the outer message is completed.
    This could lead to the creating a TVB with uninitialized heap data,
    because the fragments with NULL data are not copied but their length
    is used in the total length.

    Since add_fragment() does not create a fragment with length 0, returning
    early, the data element is always non-NULL unless the fragment has
    already been used in another reassembly. In such a case, do not re-use
    the fragment to complete a second reassembly, but leave the other
    fragments un-reassembled.

    If SCTP were able to use the standard fragment reassembly API, much of
    this would be handled and with additional expert info and warning
    fields.

    Fix #21712

    Thank to Khaled Suliman for the report and PoC.

    (cherry picked from commit 6a560feb097ca6c07ddcf97049ad3b4de7e4a5f5)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit a6e9f8bf6f
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 08:43:53 2026 -0500

    TEAP: Bound the recursion of nested TLVs

    Request-Action and Intermediate-Result TLVs, and the TLVs after an
    EAP-Payload, carry more TLVs, and dissect_teap() was called for them
    without counting the dissection depth, so nested TLVs recursed without
    a limit and could exhaust the stack. Count the depth around the calls,
    as the NAK TLV does.

