commit efbce2e76b
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 08:50:16 2026 +1000

    DNP3: Fix a loop on empty octet strings and two-pass reassembly

    An octet string object (g110/g111) with variation 0 and no prefix
    takes no space, so a 32-bit item count made the item loop run up to
    2^31 times without advancing; this took minutes of CPU per packet
    with the default ports. Reject such an object with an expert info.
    Also stop the item loop, with an expert info, when it does not
    advance for more items than a bit-packed object needs, and stop it
    after an unknown object has taken the rest of the message.

    The application layer reassembly was keyed by the IP addresses and
    the extended transport sequence number only, so messages from
    different DNP3 link addresses behind the same IP address could be
    mixed, and the per-message data was keyed by the 6-bit transport
    sequence number. Two messages in one frame with the same sequence
    number got the same reassembled message on later passes. Add the
    link addresses to the reassembly key and offset the reassembly id by
    them, as the table of reassembled messages is keyed by frame and id
    only, and key the per-message data by the index of the message in
    the frame.

    The size of the variable part of the file objects g70v4, g70v5 and
    g70v6 wrapped when the object size was smaller than the fixed fields,
    and the offset then moved back. Add an expert info and take no data.

    The 32-bit analog command events g43v1 and g43v3 did not step past
    the 32-bit value, so the item was 4 octets short and the g43v3
    timestamp was read over the value. The g70v3 file command read the
    permissions at the start of the object instead of at their own
    position. The endpoint tap built the destination endpoint from the
    link source address, so every source was shown as both transmitter
    and receiver and destinations never appeared. Signed device
    attribute values were shown unsigned in the item text (-2 as 254);
    they are now shown signed.

    [The 4.4 branch has neither the extended sequence number reassembly
    nor the DNP3 conversation and endpoint taps, so the reassembly key and
    endpoint tap changes are not part of this backport.]

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 3136e8b0280668a30cd078c6c383d0a1269fb9e4)

commit 7b581ec2f9
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 08:21:29 2026 +1000

    DOF: Fix TCP framing loop, SID learning cost and length handling

    The TCP and tunnel framing kept the start of each PDU in a 16-bit
    field, so a PDU that started 64 KiB or more into a frame was never
    found and tshark looped forever, allocating a new entry each time.
    Keep the offset in an int.

    dof.tunnel needs the packet reference of the TCP framing, so an
    Exported PDU that names it reached DISSECTOR_ASSERT(). Reject a call
    without one.

    Learning a SID walked every DOF packet seen so far, so a capture in
    which each packet carries a new SID took quadratic time. Index the
    packets by SID ID and only update those.

    A DNPv1 length shorter than the address fields wrapped around and the
    payload was taken to the end of the packet; it now gets an expert
    info. OAP Advertise passed ENC_BIG_ENDIAN as the offset of its command
    control. An OAP alias resolved to a binding from a later frame on
    later passes only. A SID longer than 255 octets was truncated when it
    was learned, and the Object ID text was built from the reported length
    of a captured buffer.

    In the security modes, lengths of domains, identities, groups and
    blocks were kept in 8 bits and truncated; a range that went backwards
    wrapped into a huge allocation. The CCM key table compared new keys
    with the cipher handle instead of the key, decryption always used the
    session cipher instead of the one for the key period, and ciphertext
    shorter than the MAC was not rejected. validate_session_key() leaked
    its HMAC handle. A preference OID nested too deep asserted, the OAP
    alias table was leaked on every file load, and an Object ID longer
    than 65535 octets was truncated.

    When a sender's provisional SID ID was merged into the SID ID of a
    known SID, the packets that had stored the provisional ID were
    updated, but the sender key still mapped to the provisional ID, so a
    later packet from that sender went back to it, without the SID. The
    sender keys are now updated as well.

    An OAP value list at the end of the PDU (a response without values, a
    Set or Invoke without parameters) threw a bounds error. An empty value
    list is now left out of the tree.

    The expert infos for reserved DNP and DPPv2 flag bits were added with
    a NULL item and did not show in the tree. They are now added to the
    flags item.

    An interface ID with length code 0 is 16 octets long in the tree, but
    its text showed no data ("[{00}:{}]"). The text now uses the same
    lengths as the tree, bounded by the stored buffer.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 1c1a917e0762a6f316ce8e6ea3095b7c05043476)

commit 8b1efebc83
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 2 10:03:47 2026 +1000

    SPOOLSS: Do not loop on key buffers or job levels, convert each string once

    The key buffer of EnumPrinterKey responses and GetCorePrinterDrivers
    requests was dissected while the offset was before an end taken from
    the packet, and a string at the end of the data returned the offset
    unchanged, so tshark looped forever. Check the size and stop when a
    string does not advance.

    EnumForms, EnumJobs and EnumPrinterDrivers responses dissected their
    entries in the buffer even when there was none, for a NULL pointer or a
    size that is too big, and hit the assertion that a tvb is given. An
    EnumJobs response with an unknown level added an expert info for every
    job without advancing, so a count of 2^32 took minutes and gigabytes.

    Each string in a buffer converted all the rest of the buffer to find
    its end, so many relative strings took quadratic time and memory: a
    64 KB EnumPrinterDrivers response took 17 seconds and 5 GB. Find the
    terminating NUL, and convert only the string; this also gives the right
    length for text that is not ASCII. Value sizes are checked before they
    are added to the offset, the printer data values stay within their
    size, and a REG_DWORD of less than 4 octets is no longer read from
    before it.

