commit 4470604636
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 08:50:16 2026 +1000

    DOCSIS: Fix MAC management TLV loop hang and fragment checks

    DCC-REQ, DCC-RSP, DCC-ACK, DCD, CM-STATUS, CM-CTRL-REQ and OCD walked
    their TLVs with a uint16_t offset. A reassembled multipart message, or
    a large frame, of more than 64 KiB made the offset wrap, so the loop
    never ended and kept adding tree items until memory ran out. Use an
    unsigned offset in these loops and in the RNG-RSP commanded power and
    OCD subcarrier assignment helpers.

    DBC-REQ and REG-RSP-MP used fragment sequence number 0 as sequence -1
    in the reassembly, and accepted numbers above the fragment count.
    Report an expert info and skip reassembly for such fragments.

    A message length below 6 made the payload length underflow. Report an
    expert info and stop after the header.

    Smaller fixes:
    - UCD OFDMA IR power control: add the two 1-byte fields with length 1
      instead of the TLV length;
    - DPD subcarrier assignment vector: the length includes the 2-byte
      start field, so do not read two bytes past the vector;
    - DPD and OCD subcarrier assignment lists: the length includes the
      type byte, so do not read an index past the list;
    - MDD diplexer band edge: continue with the next sub-TLV after a bad
      length instead of stopping, and take the override value with the
      sub-TLV length instead of always reading two bytes;
    - CWT: give the "Upstream Subcarrier Indices" subtree the sub-TLV
      length instead of the parent length;
    - CWT-RSP: show the status, not the op code, as the status in Info;
    - OPT-REQ: check the 16-bit measurement duration, not its first byte,
      against 1024.

    The BPKM attributes handed the whole message to the ASN.1 dissectors
    of public keys, certificates, signatures and OCSP responses, which
    could then read past the attribute; they now get a buffer of the
    attribute length, and an empty attribute gets an expert info. The OCSP
    response loop stops when a response does not advance. The key exchange
    share field ID and the CMTS designation CA serial number were added
    with the wrong length or offset.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit c79694d5aa1098ba208556f213099abd136fcf30)

commit 3715c2e319
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 08:00:37 2026 +1000

    WMIO: Dissect each heap entry once, do not loop on object qualifiers

    The input and output signatures of a method are objects in the method
    heap, and their classes can have methods of their own whose signatures
    point at the same objects. Every reference was followed again, so a
    chain of objects in a 2.5 KB IWbemServices response took 4^(n-1) object
    dissections and tshark never finished. Property lookups that share a
    PropertyInfo dissected it and its qualifiers again for each lookup.
    Remember the heap entries dissected in an object and show the later
    references without following them.

    The encoding length of an object qualifier is unsigned but was added as
    a signed value, so a negative length moved the offset back to the same
    qualifier and the qualifier set looped forever. Throw when the length
    is beyond the data.

    A Unicode encoded string was fetched as big endian for its label, a
    UINT8 qualifier value was read signed, the text of a UINT16 value
    replaced the label of its qualifier, and an unknown dictionary index
    showed the field index.

    Array qualifiers had the same problems: the byte size of an array of
    integers was its 32-bit count times the element size added to the
    offset unchecked, so a count near 2^32 moved the offset back to the
    same qualifier, and an array of objects added each signed object length
    and could run about 2^32 times over objects of length 0. Check the array
    size and each object length against the data.

    An Encoded-String with flags other than 0 and 1 passed a NULL string to
    "%s" for its label.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 9f424d89eb30bc72eb32950ff4c6afc87747c59d)

commit 8a1854e9f8
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 11:44:53 2026 +1000

    DCERPC: Spread policy handles over the hash, do not grow on repeated closes

    Policy handles were hashed on octets 4-7 only, so handles that share
    them all went to one bucket and a stream of such handles took quadratic
    time: 40000 LSA handles took 43 seconds.

    A close of a handle whose latest instance was already closed did not
    fall in any instance, so a new instance was allocated and inserted by
    walking the whole list. Repeated closes of one handle value grew the
    list by one entry per frame, quadratic in time: 20000 LSA Close calls
    took 17 seconds. Extend the closed instance to the repeated close
    instead, keeping the frame of the first close, so a retransmitted close
    also shows the handle it closes.

    The expert info for a SID longer than its 28-octet dom_sid28 field said
    "Association rejected".

    Assisted-by: Claude
