commit ae64e486a1
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 08:00:36 2026 +1000

    DCERPC: Do not assert on NDR64 counts, dissect deferred pointers once

    An NDR64 array max_count, offset or actual_count is 64 bits from the
    packet, and dissect_ndr_ucvarray_core(), dissect_ndr_uvarray(),
    dissect_ndr_byte_array() and dissect_ndr_vstring() checked it with
    DISSECTOR_ASSERT(val <= UINT32_MAX). Any interface used with the NDR64
    transfer syntax hit it, for example after a bind found by the TCP
    heuristic. dissect_ndr_cvstring() silently truncated the count instead.
    No array that big fits in a packet: add an expert info and throw. A
    64-bit value for a 32-bit field outside NDR64 also asserted; show it
    truncated with the same expert info.

    Deferred pointers were kept in lists that were appended to by walking
    them, indexed with g_slist_nth_data(), and searched from the bottom of
    the stack, and when the pointers of a pointed-to object were done the
    level below was scanned again from its start. Long arrays of structures
    with pointers and linked lists took quadratic or cubic time: a WINREG
    QueryMultipleValues request with 4000 nested elements took about a
    minute. Keep each level with its tail and the last pointer handled, as
    a stack with the newest level first, so that each pointer is visited
    once. Each level is also a level deeper in the tree, so a long linked
    list such as a DRSUAPI object list hit the maximum tree depth as a
    dissector bug; stop with an expert info before it.

    The hash key of every pointer was allocated in file scope on every
    pass although the table is rebuilt for each call; use the packet pool.

    dissect_ndr_ucarray_core() truncated an NDR64 max_count to 32 bits;
    check it the same way. The last fragment of a fault was added to the
    reassembly as if more fragments followed, so fault reassembly never
    completed. "-z dcerpc,srt" with an interface that has no dissector
    dereferenced NULL; report it as an invalid argument.

    The elements of an NDR array were dissected with no limit on nesting,
    and an element can hold an array of its own type. A MAPI restriction is
    dissected as an AND restriction holding an array of restrictions, so a
    65 KB RopGetSearchCriteria response overflowed the stack and crashed
    tshark. Count each array in the dissection depth.

    A count of wide characters that fits in 32 bits could still overflow
    the octet length of the string; check it as well.

    The must_check_size member of dcerpc_info is no longer used; keep it
    so that the layout of the structure does not change.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 6ae68da7a474312c8586b71c7c61a891398e8e9a)

commit 663d66c653
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 08:00:37 2026 +1000

    FRSRPC: Do not loop on a chunk size that wraps, do not assert on names

    The chunks of a CommPktChunkCtr are dissected until the end of the data,
    and each chunk adds its 32-bit size to the offset. A size near 2^32
    wrapped the offset back to the start of the chunk, so the same chunk
    was dissected forever, at 100% CPU without a tree. Stop when a chunk
    does not move the offset forward.

    The file name of a change order command was dissected as a
    NUL-terminated string followed by DISSECTOR_ASSERT() that it is shorter
    than 261 octets, so any name of 130 or more characters without a NUL
    hit the assertion. The field is a uint16 array of 261 elements, 522
    octets, but only 261 were skipped, so the padding after it was taken
    from the name. Add it as a zero-padded UTF-16 string of 522 octets.

