commit d3736a687d
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 03:15:12 2026 +1000

    DCCP: Do not wrap MP option lengths, check addresses for the checksum

    The length of an MP-DCCP option was reduced by one for the option type
    without checking that there is one, so an option with no value wrapped
    the length to 255. An MP_CONFIRM option then read past its own end and
    recursed into the following bytes, one level per 3 octets, which
    overflowed the stack and crashed tshark. With a length that cannot
    wrap, every level is inside the option above, which is at most 255
    octets. Not every MP option also stepped past the option type, so the
    next option was read one octet early. A Change or Confirm option with
    no value wrapped the length of the feature option the same way.

    The checksum pseudo-header asserted that the addresses are IPv4 or
    IPv6, which they are not in an Exported PDU naming "dccp". Do not check
    the checksum without them.

    The base of the relative sequence numbers was set when the Request or
    Response was dissected, on every pass, so the Request and the packets
    before it showed absolute numbers on the first pass and relative ones
    on the second. Set it on the first pass, before the sequence number of
    the Request is shown, and use it only from that frame on.

    The acknowledgement number of a Reset, CloseReq, Close, Sync and SyncAck
    is 48 bits at offset 2 of the subheader, but was shown as 3 octets at
    offset 1 and the relative value was masked to 24 bits. A Reset needs 12
    octets after the generic header, not 4. A CCID 3 Loss Intervals option
    was added to the options tree instead of its own.

    The IPv4 address of an MP_ADDADDR option was shown in little endian
    byte order, and the follow stream filter used the field dcp.stream.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 381391912b172329a8d2f264130083248a09c2d1)

commit 63724ac738
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 21:10:21 2026 -0500

    CP2179: Do not read more requested points than the request had

    A request keeps the points it asked for, but not how many there are,
    and the special calculation range and 16-bit analog scan responses
    indexed that list with a counter that only stopped at the end of the
    response. A response with more values than the request read past the
    heap buffer, and a request with no points has a NULL list, which
    crashed tshark. Both are reached with an Exported PDU naming "cp2179".
    Keep the number of points and stop there. Also do not wrap the count
    when the end point of a scan is below the start point.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>

    (cherry picked from commit 516257e00cbb49da91d479fe26cc999558ef4d8c)

