commit 3daa4a5e59
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 21:09:20 2026 -0500

    CSN.1: Do not write more elements than a variable array holds

    The element count of an M_VAR_TARRAY or M_VAR_TARRAY_OFFSET comes from
    the stream, and the elements were stored in the decoded structure with
    no check against the array size. In GSM RLC/MAC, an EXT Measurement
    Report can claim 31 measurements for an array of 9, and NC, CCN and 3G
    measurement reports 7 or 8 for arrays of 6, so the decoder wrote past
    the array into the rest of the uplink message structure. Store the array
    size in the descriptor, as M_REC_TARRAY does, and stop with an error
    when the count is larger. In a union, a count of 0 for the offset
    variant also no longer wraps to 65535.

    An M_REC_ARRAY element was always read with tvb_get_bits8(), which
    asserts for more than 8 bits. GSM RLC/MAC uses M_REC_ARRAY for 9 to 16
    bit PCIDs, PSCs, UTRAN FDD and TDD ARFCNs and EARFCNs, which are kept in
    uint16_t arrays, so a Packet Cell Change Order or Packet Measurement
    Order with any of them stopped with a dissector bug. Store those
    elements in the uint16_t array.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>

    (cherry picked from commit 1911eb17b02bc4af4273884ae57d108408af447c)

    Co-authored-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>

commit 4fad117218
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 31 10:18:49 2026 +1000

    DCT2000: Do not crash or hang on malformed records

    A PDCP key comment that matches the sscanf conversions but omits the
    "key: " literal still returns 3. strstr() is then NULL and g_strdup()
    of that pointer crashes. Hex after a MAC-NR, NRUP, or RawTraffic '$'
    was decoded two digits at a time while testing only the first byte, so
    an odd length stepped over the NUL and read or wrote past the buffer.
    With no '$', the MAC-NR decoder treated the header text as payload and
    the NRUP decoder started one byte past the NUL.

    An Exported PDU record can name this dissector with any encapsulation
    byte and without a Catapult DCT2000 pseudo-header. An unknown byte hit
    DISSECTOR_ASSERT_NOT_REACHED(). ATM and ISDN read that pseudo-header,
    and PPP took its direction from the same union. The direction now comes
    from the record, and the inner header is used only for a real DCT2000
    file. ISDN without that header is handed to LAPD.

    Any fp_r* name other than fp_r4 through fp_r8 took the same assert.
    Channel, transport-block, transport-format, and paging counts were
    copied into fp_info and used as loop bounds by the FP dissector, with
    or without a tree. A count above INT_MAX became negative and skipped
    the channel cap. Counts are limited before they are stored: 64
    channels, 512 transport blocks, and sizes that cannot overflow the FP
    length arithmetic.

    An IPPrim remote-address TLV whose length was not 4 or 16 bytes was
    stored as an IPv6 address of that length. Only 4-byte and 16-byte
    addresses are installed.

    Assisted-by: Grok
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 1ac7497d30985d87b0eaa287809fc413625aca0c)

commit 071564b3e2
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 30 16:35:10 2026 +1000

    CMIP: Do not use an object identifier from an earlier packet

    The object identifier of an action, event, error or extension type is
    kept in a file scope pointer to a string in packet scope, and the
    information that follows uses it to pick a dissector even when the
    packet did not set it, for example for an action type in local form.
    It then read the string of an earlier packet after it was freed.
    Keep it with the packet instead, clear it at the start of each CMIP
    PDU, and restore the one of an outer PDU at the end.

    Assisted-by: Claude
