commit 7dbc4832fe
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 25 00:11:08 2026 -0400

    RTMPT: Free the data of a message replaced before it is complete

    When a new message header arrives on a chunk stream whose previous
    message is still incomplete, the previous message is abandoned: it is
    never added to the list of complete messages, so its data is never
    dissected, but its buffer stayed allocated in file scope until the
    capture file was closed. Nothing limits how many such messages there
    can be.

    Free the buffer when the message is replaced, and mark the message as
    complete so that nothing is appended to it later.

    Ping #21677

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5

    (cherry picked from commit c0a0d515060a305e492cf3beea5845f8f2adf364)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit 3a47906a5b
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 24 23:42:55 2026 -0400

    RTMPT: Allocate only the header when starting to unchunk a message

    Each RTMP message whose body is longer than the chunk size started with
    a 32 KiB file scope allocation (or the declared length, if smaller).
    Every message on a chunk stream can declare a 24-bit body length and
    send as little as one byte of body before the next message header, so
    a 12 byte message header with a Set Chunk Size of 1 allocated 32 KiB
    that was never freed, about 2500 times the input.

    Allocate just the message header to start. The buffer is already grown
    as the body arrives, doubling each time, so the memory now grows with
    the bytes actually received.

    Fix #21677

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5

    (cherry picked from commit dc7d00150a4219c26c8b21ff27c7589c08e510e4)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit d77a77b905
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 24 23:23:30 2026 -0400

    9P: Stop building a Twalk path after the first 250 names

    The Twalk loop stopped advancing the offset after 250 path elements
    but kept appending to the fid path for every one of the up to 65535
    elements in nwname. Each extra pass re-read the 251st element, so a
    Twalk of about 60 KB with a large last name grew the path by gigabytes
    on the first pass and aborted tshark when the allocation failed.

    Stop the whole loop at 250 elements, as Rwalk already does.

