commit e3987ad6ca
Author: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 18:05:59 2026 +1000

    SMB: Fix memory safety issues found in an audit

    - SRT tap: the Trans2 and NT Transact subcommands are 16-bit values
      from the packet but indexed a 256 entry table. add_srt_table_data()
      only checks the index with ws_assert(), so release builds wrote past
      the end of the heap buffer. Skip out-of-range subcommands.
    - Export objects: avoid a division by zero when a file has length 0.
    - AndX chains recursed through dissect_smb_command() without a depth
      limit. Use increment_dissection_depth().
    - User quota lists: a quota entry offset could point backwards and
      make the loop run forever when no tree is built. Require each entry
      to move forward and stay within the byte count.
    - DFS referrals: report an expert info and stop when a referral
      string, or its offset, extends past the end of the transaction
      data, instead of letting the byte count wrap around.
    - FIND_FIRST2/FIND_NEXT2: stop when an entry does not advance.
    - Zero-initialize file-scope FID and conversation structures whose
      fields could otherwise be read uninitialized.

    Assisted-by: Claude
    Signed-off-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
    (backported from commit 04f1fa8cb816126e91109d033516291d99b78116)

commit d95c695a28
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 23 09:33:42 2026 -0400

    wslua: Check that TvbRanges have not expired before using them

    The TreeItem methods add(), add_le(), add_packet_field() and
    add_tvb_expert_info() accepted a TvbRange without checking whether it
    had expired, as did TvbRange:le_float() and the TvbRange __eq
    metamethod. A Lua script that kept a TvbRange from a previous packet
    (e.g., in a global or upvalue) and passed it to one of these would
    read from the tvbuff of the earlier packet after it was freed.

    Raise an "expired tvb" error in those cases, as the other TvbRange
    methods already do.

