commit d95a611941
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Oct 15 08:24:35 2026 -0400

    BT HSP, BT HFP: Bound AT command reassembly

    An AT command or response that never ends kept growing the same
    reassembly chain without limit. The unsigned offset of each fragment
    could in principle wrap (after 4 GiB), making the reassembly buffer
    smaller than the fragments copied into it, and every terminator
    character in a frame walked back through the entire chain to find its
    first fragment, which is quadratic in the length of the chain.

    Start a new message once the reassembled length would exceed 64 KiB,
    and store the first fragment of each chain instead of searching for it.
    V.250 and the HSP/HFP specifications don't limit the length of an AT
    command or response; this limit is arbitrary, well above any real
    command line, chosen to keep the offsets from overflowing.

    Thanks to AISLE Security for the report

    Fix #21653

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

    (cherry picked from commit d277f7c5c21215dd16b06a59d941e54193095679)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit 4f07e6dcd6
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 14 21:14:35 2026 -0400

    radiotap: Don't allow remaining length of EHT TLV to wrap

    Retrieve the EHT TLV length (an unsigned 16 bit integer) but store it
    in a signed int so that the remaining length becomes negative instead
    of wrapping (whether the original length is too short or if the length
    isn't a multiple of 4.) This stops the loop at the check instead of
    dissecting later TLVs and the 802.11 frame itself as EHT user info
    fields.

    We don't implement true proper handling of short TLVs according to the radiotap
    documentation, which says "Parsers shall be able to parse TLV fields with
    partial data present as indicated by the length of the data, i.e. producers
    can omit bytes they don’t fill at the end of the data. Such bytes are assumed
    to be 0." It's not clear any producers do that, though.

    Ping #21661

    (cherry picked from commit aae6da75c524dc8525373ae1a2bd634592e0e278)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit 5d893f339e
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Oct 12 23:49:45 2026 -0400

    Aethra: Discard the data of skipped records on the first pass

    The Aethra wiretap module intends to ignore records that are not
    LAPD frames. However, when reading sequentially, the skipped records
    data are read into the record Buffer and kept, so the next frame
    returned has a Buffer which starts with all the skipped record data
    concatenated before its own (but with a caplen in the record taken
    from its own value.) On later passes, when wtap_seek_read is used,
    the record is retrieved correctly because the file_offset is still
    updated for each record, so this is most visible on one-pass TShark
    output, which differs from two-pass TShark and Wireshark view.

    Clean the buffer at the end of the loop if not breaking out of the loop.

    Thanks to AISLE Security for observing the issue (it is not really a
    security issue, because the amount of excess memory allocated to the
    first record is still no greater than what is contained in the file.)

    Fix #21654

    (backported from commit f1c00f80182d6ea20793736a83e9569282df985d)

