commit b87b352aaf
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Oct 12 21:25:20 2026 -0400

    BT L2CAP: Don't underflow the remaining LE SDU length

    In LE Credit Based Flow Control mode, if the first frame of an SDU
    declares an SDU length shorter than its payload, or a later frame is
    longer than the remaining length, the unsigned remaining length
    wrapped. Every later frame on the channel was then treated as a
    continuation of an SDU that never completes, and added to the reassembly.

    Treat a first frame whose payload is at least the SDU length as a
    complete SDU (with an expert info if the SDU length is too short),
    and a continuation frame that is at least the remaining length as the
    last one.

    A first frame whose L2CAP length is less than 2 is too short for the
    SDU length field. Previously, if more bytes were captured than the
    length, it started a segmented SDU and the 16-bit length minus 2
    wrapped, so the following frames were swallowed as continuations.
    If fewer were captured, reading the SDU length threw on the first pass
    before the frame data was stored, so the second pass dissected the frame
    differently. Mark such frames as too short, end any SDU in progress,
    and show them as payload with an expert info, before reading anything
    from the tvb.

    Thanks to AISLE Security for the report.

    Fix #21648

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

    (cherry picked from commit 828c0f2b42a2259af29c7444af9b57d026256d18)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit a7d654df7f
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 11 11:28:53 2026 -0400

    Kerberos: Check the PAC signature buffer lengths

    When verifying a PAC, the checksum type was read from the first four
    bytes of the server, KDC, ticket and full signature buffers without
    checking their length. krb5_pac_parse() accepts a zero length buffer,
    and krb5_pac_get_buffer() then returns a one byte allocation, so a
    malformed PAC caused a heap over-read.

    Only use a signature buffer that is long enough to hold the checksum
    type. Later code already checks the length of the checksum itself.

    This requires Kerberos decryption to be enabled and a build with MIT
    Kerberos PAC verification.

    Thanks to AISLE Research for the report

    Fix #21644

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    (backported from commit f57fdc80f23b0fc4f2bdffbc03b754632eb0c814)

commit 61ddb9deba
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Oct 12 20:59:10 2026 -0400

    BT SDP: Don't copy all the continuation data for each response

    Each response in a continuation sequence allocated a new file scope
    buffer for all the attribute list bytes so far plus its own, and kept
    all the previous ones, so memory grew quadratically with the number of
    responses (even responses with no attribute list bytes made a new copy
    of everything). The total length was also an unchecked 32-bit sum, and
    the length was stored before the copy from the tvb, which could throw
    and leave uninitialized bytes to be dissected on later passes.

    Store each response's bytes once, linked to the previous responses',
    and assemble the attribute list in packet scope when dissecting.

    Limit the total to 1 MiB. Each response's AttributeListByteCount is
    16 bits, but the specification does not limit the total across
    continuation responses; this limit is arbitrary, chosen to keep the
    total length from overflowing and to bound the buffer allocated when
    the final response is dissected.

    Thanks to Aisle Research for reporting.

    Fix #21649

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

    (cherry picked from commit d7fefcbe390db22d3bc93ed33e7ff36f2f1c8b5a)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

