commit 07260ac4a2
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 11 18:27:43 2026 -0400

    k12 text: Don't match an unlimited number of hours digits

    The Tektronix K12 text format stores a time of day field at the
    beginning of each frame. The lexer should not match an unlimited number
    of digits for the hours token, as then a file which matches the magic
    bytes at the beginning can read for an extremely long time, taking up
    memory and CPU time and creating a Denial of Service.

    Limit the hours fields to 2 digits at maximum, treating earlier digits
    as junk. The parser could be made stricter against errors (or could
    allow three or four digit hours, if that's actually possible in the
    format, so long as unlimited is not allowed.)

    Thanks to AISLE Research for the report and PoC.

    (cherry picked from commit 78ead179aa68c0e0adfb4e9ff6b81f65657e5655)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit 2e36691493
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 11 15:41:34 2026 -0400

    IEEE 1609.2: Handle gmtime() failure when formatting times

    The Time32 and Time64 formatters passed the result of gmtime()
    straight to snprintf. gmtime() returns NULL for times outside the
    range the C library can represent; on Windows that is anything after
    the year 3000, which a Time64 from packet data easily exceeds, so the
    dissector crashed when building the tree.

    Use ws_gmtime_r() and show "Not representable" when it fails.

    Thanks to Aisle Security for the report.

    Fix #21640

    (cherry picked from commit e13e9f9046ddf2f2c66e193ff9cc733583efac81)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit 8c18d52e03
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 11 11:24:43 2026 -0400

    logcat text: Don't match an unread line buffer when opening

    logcat_text_open() reads lines into a g_malloc'd buffer until it
    gets one that isn't short or special, and then matches the buffer
    against the formats. If no line could be read at all, as for an
    empty file, the buffer had never been written, and matching it read
    uninitialized memory, possibly past the end of the buffer. Return
    "not mine" (or the read error) in that case.

