commit b1668088ec
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 11 08:12:46 2026 -0400

    nettrace: Don't preallocate an INT_MAX-sized buffer

    nettrace_3gpp_32_423_file_open() reserved RINGBUFFER_START_SIZE ==
    INT_MAX for the streaming buffer. g_array_sized_new() rounds that up to
    the next power of two, so it is really a 2 GiB g_realloc(), for a buffer
    that read_until() then fills 1024 bytes at a time. glib treats an
    allocation failure as fatal, so where the request cannot be satisfied
    the process aborts during file open, before any packet is parsed. Any
    XML file with a <traceCollecFile> root reaches this.

    Reserve 64 KiB instead and let the append path grow the buffer.

    Impact on 64-bit systems is low: the mapping is lazy, so the untouched
    2 GiB never becomes resident, and such machines generally have well over
    2 GiB of RAM anyway. It matters on 32-bit builds and under an address
    space limit (RLIMIT_AS, cgroups) or without overcommit, where the
    allocation genuinely fails and glib aborts. It was also a waste for
    legitimate traces, as the reservation is never released.

    Thanks to Aisle Research for the report.

    Fixes #21507

    (cherry picked from commit 008a0dacdb78b3e05bdb30c6fc0b2228028faed8)

    Co-authored-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>

