commit 7f16cd62f0
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 10 10:01:53 2026 -0400

    logcat text: Check binary entries before writing them as text

    When converting binary logcat records to a text format, the
    dumper trusted the entry header: it didn't check that the record
    held the header and the payload, it found the end of the tag with
    strlen, and it computed the message length as payload length minus
    the tag length. The binary reader only validates the first three
    entries of a file, so a later entry with a short payload or a tag
    without a terminating NUL made the dumper read past the record,
    or pass a negative length to g_strndup and abort.

    Check the record against the header and payload lengths, and find
    the end of the tag within the payload.

    Fix #21636

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    (backported from commit 87e96a1d1c1c66cfbbacec06c04883bc434272ca)

commit 75021b2094
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 10 17:45:21 2026 -0400

    ERF: Use the Module snaplen as the interface default

    When an interface section doesn't have its snaplen set, the code checked
    to see if the module section had a snaplen. However, instead of using
    the snaplen from the module if set in that case, it interpreted the last
    tag of the interface section as the snaplen, which at best is the wrong
    value or at worst could be past the end of the record by 4 or fewer
    bytes. Correctly use the saved module snaplen, similar to what is done
    for the FCS length.

    Most of the time the negative effects are reading possibly uninitialzed
    bytes from the Buffer (also possibly leftover from the previous record),
    though it may be possible to construct a case that reads past the
    allocated Buffer data itself.

    Fix #21635

    Thanks to AISLE Security for the report and proposed fix.

    (backported from commit bec0686a0e50482bbac24861e603187ee42f8725)

commit 817f931028
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 10 14:22:32 2026 -0400

    observer: Return -1 for failure when packet data cannot be read

    Similar to 7be61b1f22b7f713814b3952ed2e37a1ef1984f5 but for Observer.
    The calling functions expect a negative value for failure, not zero,
    which is treated as success with zero bytes (which have not been
    read into the buffer despite caplen having a positive value.)

    Most of the time this results in copying uninitialized data. It might
    be possible to craft a unusual file that tries to copy outside the
    Buffer, but current master or release-4.6 does have a check for this.

    Thanks to AISLE Security for the report.

    (backported from commit 279f438a04d2a75e75256725260edba7edde3574)

commit cc59e57a90
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 10 08:16:25 2026 -0400

    NBAP, UMTS FP: Don't write past the per-channel arrays

    The NBAP DCH chain loops checked num_dch_in_flow < FP_maxNrOfDCHs
    before incrementing it and using it as an index, and then wrote one
    more entry after the loop, so a flow with the maximum number of DCHs
    wrote past dch_ids_in_flow_list and fp_dch_channel_info. Stop one
    entry earlier and remove the extra write, which nothing reads.

    The number of UL and DL transport formats per channel was incremented
    for each item, but not every TransportFormatSet resets it, so it could
    exceed MAX_FP_CHANS. Set it from the (bounded) item count instead.

    UMTS FP's per-channel arrays only have MAX_FP_CHANS entries, fewer
    than the number of DCHs NBAP allows in a flow, so check the count in
    the consumer too, and clamp the channel count read from K12 files.

    Thanks to AISLE Research for the report.

    Fix #21630

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

    (backported from commit 6d2bd70dc4a6f46bf094a5888b33596b003c9b63)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

