commit fc30b73244
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 9 20:49:06 2026 -0400

    RTPS: Fix OOB write in DATA_BATCH sample info list

    dissect_RTPS_DATA_BATCH() allocates sample_info_flags[] and
    sample_info_length[] with sample_info_max entries, then fills them in the
    sample-info loop indexed by sample_info_count. The loop's bounds check
    only fired when the max_batch_samples_dissected preference was > 0:

      if (rtps_max_batch_samples_dissected > 0 && ... >= rtps_max_batch_samples_dissected)

    When the preference is set to 0 (a valid value meaning "show up to 1024"),
    sample_info_max is set to 1024 for the allocation, but the guard is
    disabled entirely. A crafted DATA_BATCH submessage with many minimal
    sample-info records (~8 bytes each) then drives sample_info_count past
    1024, writing sample_info_flags[] and sample_info_length[] out of bounds
    in packet-scope (wmem) memory, and the stale entries are read again when
    the batched samples are dissected.

    Guard on sample_info_max instead, which equals the preference when it is
    > 0 and 1024 when it is 0, so the loop can never exceed the allocation in
    either case.

    This requires the non-default preference value of 0; with the default
    (16) the OOB write is not reachable.

    Reported by Aisle Research.

    Fixes #21508

    (cherry picked from commit 716a200295d2f91629211fd8660e8cce37c13884)

    Co-authored-by: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>

commit a57511ba14
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 9 20:47:11 2026 -0400

    ERF: Bound metadata dump scan by captured length, not wlen

    erf_dump_priv_compare_capture_comment() walked the Provenance (META)
    record's tags using the record's on-wire length (pseudo_header->erf.phdr.wlen)
    as the remaining-bytes bound, while pd points to a buffer holding only
    caplen (= rlen - header) bytes. A record whose wlen exceeds its captured
    payload therefore made erf_meta_read_tag() read past the end of the
    buffer. wlen is attacker-controlled (16-bit, up to 65535) and is not
    constrained to caplen on read.

    Use the captured length instead, matching the read side
    (populate_summary_info() already parses metadata using the rlen-derived
    packet_size).

    The read path is safe, and stock tshark/editcap do not reach this writer
    code when re-exporting ERF (they copy IDBs, which the ERF writer rejects,
    aborting before any packet record is written). It is reachable from any
    libwiretap consumer that writes ERF without copying IDBs, e.g. via
    wtap_dump_params_init_no_idbs(): feeding a crafted ERF META record
    (caplen 8, wlen 65535, one Capture Section tag) through wtap_dump() to an
    ERF dumper produces tens of thousands of invalid reads under Valgrind in
    erf_meta_read_tag(); none after this change.

    Thanks to AISLE Security for reporting

    Fixes: #21629

    Assisted-by: Claude Opus 4.8

    (cherry picked from commit 7cb4b5ca791f8ed2457392ca90e6cfa9f275be13)

    Co-authored-by: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>

commit 036df7a350
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 9 16:02:46 2026 -0400

    Kerberos: Trim the saved key's keylength when truncating it

    While the largest key length we expect to get in valid data is 32 bytes
    (e.g., AES-256), this is obtained from cleartext packet data and a BER
    ASN.1 OCTET STRING with no constraint  If we truncate the key bytes when
    storing them to KRB_MAX_KEY_LENGTH, record the truncated length in the
    stored key, similar to what is done in keytab_file_read in read_keytab_file.c
    The keylength can be used for hashing and comparisons using the bytes,
    and thus risks memory-safety errors otherwise.

    Fixup 20bfb9ffb90cac37892630e1c24743c8b6800471
    Fix #21625

