commit 052b5d3c1a
Author: John Thacker <johnthacker@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 30 10:42:35 2026 -0400

    K12 Text: Use a GString for writing

    We already set a limit on the maximum frame we are willing to write,
    based on WTAP_MAX_PACKET_SIZE_STANDARD, but the fixed output buffer
    allocated is not large enough to handle such a size. Compounding the
    issue, the return values for snprintf are not correctly handled in
    the case of insufficient room.

    Instead of a fixed buffer and snprintf, allocate a GString with
    sufficient capacity based upon the caplen and the header and footer.
    libwiretap is able to read this maximum length; if this is too large
    for some third party purposes, the maximum should be reduced elsewhere.

    Fix #21606

    (backported from commit 3729b649680e3ff878e128a6e39d236020d870fb)

commit 6cdb6dcd0f
Author: Anders Broman <a.broman58@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Sep 28 11:26:34 2026 +0200

    OPCUA: Use built-in ExtensionObject parsers only for ns 0

    getExtensionObjectType() returned only the numeric part of the TypeId
    and dropped the namespace, so an ExtensionObject of a custom type whose
    numeric ID matches a standard encoding ID was decoded with the wrong
    built-in parser, e.g. ns=1;i=886 silently as Range. Return 0 (no
    built-in parser) for TypeIds outside namespace 0; such bodies are then
    shown as ByteString, like string, Guid and ByteString TypeIds already
    are.

    Also fix the NodeId encoding comments (0x04 is Guid, 0x05 ByteString,
    there is no 0x06) and add the first OPC UA suite test.

